-- title:
--   The Hamiltonian isotopy of $Q_3$: rotation followed by the midpoint perturbation
-- statement:
--   On $\mathbb{C}^5$ write $q(z)=\sum_jz_j^2$, $|z|^2=\sum_j|z_j|^2$ and $\omega(u,v)=2\operatorname{Im}\sum_j\bar u_jv_j$. The unit quadric is $S=\{z:|z|^2=1,\ q(z)=0\}$, and $Q_3=\{[z]\in\mathbb{CP}^4:q(z)=0\}=S/U(1)$. Let $R_t$ be the double rotation by angle $\pi t$ in the $(z_1,z_2)$ and $(z_3,z_4)$ planes, generated by $X_{\mathrm{rot}}$ with Hamiltonian $H_{\mathrm{rot}}$. Let $F_{\mathrm{mid}},G_{\mathrm{mid}},H_{\mathrm{mid}}$ be the forward map, backward map and Hamiltonian of the implicit-midpoint isotopy of the compact field.
--
--   1. **Rotations are symplectic.** $R_t$, viewed as a real-linear map, preserves $\omega$. Hence $\omega(R_tu,v)=\omega(u,R_{-t}v)$. For a differentiable curve $u(s)$,
--   $$
--   \frac{d}{ds}\Big|_{s=t}R_s\bigl(u(s)\bigr)=X_{\mathrm{rot}}\bigl(R_tu(t)\bigr)+R_t\,u'(t).
--   $$
--   2. **The isotopy.** Define
--   $$
--   F(t,z)=R_t\bigl(F_{\mathrm{mid}}(t,z)\bigr),\quad G(t,z)=G_{\mathrm{mid}}\bigl(t,R_{-t}z\bigr),\quad H(t,z)=H_{\mathrm{rot}}(z)+H_{\mathrm{mid}}\bigl(t,R_{-t}z\bigr).
--   $$
--   They have the following properties.
--      - $F$, $G$ and $H$ are smooth on $\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{C}^5$.
--      - $F(t,\cdot)$ and $G(t,\cdot)$ preserve $S$, commute with unit complex scalars, and are mutually inverse; $H$ is phase invariant.
--      - $F(0,\cdot)=\mathrm{id}$.
--      - For all $t$, $z\in S$ and $v\in\mathbb{C}^5$,
--   $$
--   \omega\bigl(\partial_tF(t,z),v\bigr)=d_wH\bigl(t,F(t,z)\bigr)[v].
--   $$
--   3. **The main map.** $\varphi:Q_3\to Q_3$ is the bijection obtained by descending $F(1,\cdot)$ and $G(1,\cdot)$, so that $\varphi([z])=[F(1,z)]$. Together with $F$, $G$ and $H$, this is a Hamiltonian path for $\varphi$ in the sense of the mission statement.
--
--   $\varphi$ is the coordinate involution of $Q_3$ followed by a small Hamiltonian perturbation. It is the Hamiltonian bijection of the main theorem.
--
--   **Formalization Note** `mainHamiltonianPath : HamiltonianPath mainEquiv` packages all the required fields. The Hamiltonian equation is proved for every $v$, not only for horizontal ones.
-- source:
